  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "If the sixth term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{16}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{4},$ then the third term of this sequence is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{4},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $6$th term is ${a}_{6}=16,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{6-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{4}^{2}} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{{4}^{5}}{{4}^{2}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=64,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{2}} \\\\  & ={4}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third term  is ${a}_{3}=4.$",
